Psoriatic Arthritis Axial Involvement
Complex Fatigue Entities
Inflammation of the sacroiliac joints and spine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-042
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe spinal rigidity, excruciating back pain, and complete functional immobility.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 24-hour escalation of debilitating spinal pain resulting in an inability to weight-bear or walk. Immediate on-site assessment was required to definitively rule out acute spinal cord compression, cauda equina syndrome, or acute vertebral collapse, and to provide potent pharmacological relief prior to standard clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My spine feels like it's fused solid with cement, the pain is burning through my lower back, and I absolutely cannot walk.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known Psoriatic Arthritis reports a severe, acute flare localized to the axial skeleton. The current episode began 48 hours ago with profound morning stiffness lasting nearly 5 hours, evolving into severe inflammatory back pain that paradoxically improves slightly with movement but is agonizing at rest. The patient is experiencing severe, unrelenting bilateral sacroiliac pain radiating into the buttocks. Accompanying fatigue is severe, described as an inability to keep eyes open.
Functional Status: Temporarily bedbound; unable to flex the lumbar spine or perform sit-to-stand transfers safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high fall risk due to axial rigidity and pain-induced weakness in the lower limbs.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 94 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, lying perfectly flat on their back, resisting any rolling or twisting movements.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam shows mild psoriatic plaques on the elbows. Lumbar spine exam reveals severe paraspinal muscle spasm and near-zero lumbar flexion (modified Schober test heavily restricted). Exquisite tenderness to direct palpation and compression over the bilateral sacroiliac joints. Lower limb neurological exam shows intact reflexes, normal distal sensation, and 5/5 motor strength. Perianal sensation is intact. Clinical presentation is classic for severe inflammation of the sacroiliac joints and spine without cord compromise.
Environmental Observations: Floor rugs and low lighting observed between the bed and the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Took maximum daily doses of Paracetamol and Ibuprofen safely with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Psoriatic Arthritis Axial Involvement - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact bowel/bladder control, normal perianal sensation, and absence of saddle anesthesia.

Acute Disc Herniation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral radiating leg pain (sciatica) below the knee, and normal straight leg raise test.

Mechanical Lumbar Strain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of severe morning stiffness, pain that improves with movement rather than rest, and localized sacroiliitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of severe axial immobility inherently carries the risk of occult neurological compromise. An urgent after-hours home visit was absolutely necessary to perform a targeted neurological examination to exclude surgical emergencies like cauda equina syndrome. Once confirmed as a severe neuro-inflammatory axial flare of PsA, the patient required immediate, prescription-strength anti-inflammatory administration in the home setting, as standard analgesics had failed, leaving the patient immobilized in severe distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM to deliver immediate, potent suppression of sacroiliac inflammation. Prescribed Meloxicam 15mg daily. Targeted intervention strategy includes COX-2 inhibitors combined with Cannabinoids, and Omega-3.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking a hot shower immediately upon waking tomorrow and performing gentle, supported spinal extensions, you will physically dilate the inflamed blood vessels in your spine, directly reducing the morning rigidity and rewarding yourself with improved daily mobility.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Recommended using a firm mattress and avoiding low chairs.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to coordinate rheumatology review and consider biologic escalation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the patient develops numbness in the groin/saddle area, sudden loss of bowel or bladder control, or sudden weakness in the legs.

